    {
      "id": "exam/edexcel-9ma0-32-2024-june/q5",
      "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:110mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/exams/edexcel-9ma0-32-2024-june/q04-figure.svg\" alt=\"Figure 4. Path of P from O to A: a symmetric arc above horizontal ground, starting at O with velocity 35 m s^-1 at angle alpha above the horizontal and landing at A.\"></figure><p>At time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t=0\"></span>, a small stone is projected with velocity <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"35\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}\"></span> from a point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span> on horizontal ground.</p><p>The stone is projected at an angle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\alpha\"></span> to the horizontal, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tan\\alpha=\\dfrac{3}{4}\"></span></p><p>In an initial model</p><ul><li>the stone is modelled as a particle <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> moving freely under gravity</li><li>the stone hits the ground at the point <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span></li></ul><p>Figure 4 shows the path of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span> to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span>.</p><p>For the motion of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span> to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span></p><ul><li>at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\"></span> seconds, the horizontal distance of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> from <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"O\"></span> is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span> metres</li><li>at time <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"t\"></span> seconds, the vertical distance of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"P\"></span> above the ground is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span> metres</li></ul><ol class=\"parts\"><li>Using the model, show that<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"y=\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{1}{160}x^2\" data-display=\"true\"></div><span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></li><li>Use the answer to (a), or otherwise, to find the length <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"OA\"></span>. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li></ol><p>Using the model, the greatest height of the stone above the ground is found to be <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"H\"></span> metres.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"3\"><li>Use the answer to (a), or otherwise, to find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"H\"></span>. <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li></ol><ul><li>The model is refined to include air resistance.</li></ul><p>Using this new model, the greatest height of the stone above the ground is found to be <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"K\"></span> metres.</p><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"4\"><li>State which is greater, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"H\"></span> or <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"K\"></span>, justifying your answer. <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li><li>State one limitation of this refined model. <span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li></ol>",
